<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Ieyasu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1543">1543</a>–<a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ieyasu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Ieyasu was a Japanese warlord who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>. After his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, he was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> by the imperial court in 1603, inaugurating two and a half centuries of Tokugawa rule. Accounts of the Tokugawa Shogunate describe how he built a system of control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> that brought lasting peace to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after a long period of civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Ieyasu" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanō Tan'yū · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Ieyasu2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1609 the Shimazu clan, with the consent of <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b>, sent about 3,000 samurai armed with arquebuses in more than 100 ships under Kabayama Hisataka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tokugawa Shogunate, or Tokugawa bakufu, was established in 1603 when <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b> was appointed Sei-i Taishogun ('barbarian-subduing generalissimo') by the imperial court in Kyoto, inaugurating two and a half centuries of Tokugawa rule over Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…sun, the wind and thunder; mountains such as Fuji and Miwa, waterfalls, rocks and ancient trees; the ancestors of clans and families; and outstanding human beings, such as the scholar Sugawara no Michizane, worshipped as Tenjin, the kami of learning, and the shogun <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b>, enshrined at Nikko.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>At Nagashino in 1575 the massed arquebusiers of Oda Nobunaga and <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b> broke the charges of the Takeda cavalry, and the warlords who mastered firearms went on to unify the country.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>The office was hereditary in the Tokugawa house: fifteen shoguns, all descended from <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b>, held it from 1603 to 1867, and when the main line failed, the related houses of Owari, Kii and Mito could supply an heir.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>Ishida Mitsunari's Western Army laid siege to Fushimi Castle, held for <b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b> by a much smaller garrison under Torii Mototada.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Okazaki Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Sunpu Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.16&amp;year=1590" aria-label="Okazaki Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8027 899 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tokugawa Ieyasu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8477 1169h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8477 1169h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.16&amp;year=1590">What was here in 1590? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
